{"title":"2012 AMC 8 Problems/Problem 14","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><div id=\"toc\" class=\"toc\" role=\"navigation\" aria-labelledby=\"mw-toc-heading\"><input type=\"checkbox\" role=\"button\" id=\"toctogglecheckbox\" class=\"toctogglecheckbox\" style=\"display:none\" /><div class=\"toctitle\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2 id=\"mw-toc-heading\">Contents</h2><span class=\"toctogglespan\"><label class=\"toctogglelabel\" for=\"toctogglecheckbox\"></label></span></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-1\"><a href=\"#Problem\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-2\"><a href=\"#Solution_1\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 1</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-3\"><a href=\"#Solution_2\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 2</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-4\"><a href=\"#Solution_3\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">4</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 3</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-5\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">5</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-6\"><a href=\"#See_Also\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">6</span> <span class=\"toctext\">See Also</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2012_AMC_8_Problems/Problem_14&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>In the BIG N, a middle school football conference, each team plays every other team exactly once. If a total of 21 conference games were played during the 2012 season, how many teams were members of the BIG N conference?\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/7/f/e7f7f03d4d6bb2572be2af16a613e47fb9030b43.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\textbf{(A)}\\hspace{.05in}6\\qquad\\textbf{(B)}\\hspace{.05in}7\\qquad\\textbf{(C)}\\hspace{.05in}8\\qquad\\textbf{(D)}\\hspace{.05in}9\\qquad\\textbf{(E)}\\hspace{.05in}10$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"360\" height=\"17\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_1\">Solution 1</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2012_AMC_8_Problems/Problem_14&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 1\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>This problem is very similar to a handshake problem. We use the formula <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/3/4/53404ad4af12519f95b03c74a1c7af0d609baa8e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{n(n-1)}{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"69\" height=\"38\" /> to usually find the number of games played (or handshakes). Now we have to use the formula in reverse.\n</p><p>So we have the equation  <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/7/3/d73d435bf904a14cd7160c2e2da66670a8b006cd.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{n(n-1)}{2} = 21$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"112\" height=\"38\" />. Solving, we find that the number of teams in the BIG N conference is  <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/8/2/382b34f2212ff1f84f196b1f33016258e2aa6e24.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{\\textbf{(B)}\\ 7}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -10px\" width=\"54\" height=\"28\" />.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_2\">Solution 2</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2012_AMC_8_Problems/Problem_14&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 2\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>(If someone understands what I'm trying to do here and can explain it better, please edit it)We know that every team has to play a game with every other team, so we just need to find out how many consecutive numbers, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" />, can fit into <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/2/5/f25d31faf472ddb4c698a14522fe8125c06f8dd8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$21$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"14\" />. We know that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/2/a/62ad113fe64fde10385c25c27a9e3c0f45b38a6f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$6+5+4+3+2+1=21$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"207\" height=\"14\" />, and since this doesn't count to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/f/e/dfecd48604f4d56df72d59786de714456b3d3c47.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$7th$\" width=\"25\" height=\"12\" /> team that shook hands with the other <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/0/1/601a7806cbfad68196c43a4665871f8c3186802e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$6$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />, we know that there are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/8/2/382b34f2212ff1f84f196b1f33016258e2aa6e24.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{\\textbf{(B)}\\ 7}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -10px\" width=\"54\" height=\"28\" /> teams in the BIG N conference.\n</p><p>EDIT: So basically reiterating this, we are using a factorial and adding one because the team that they are talking about is not shaking the hands. Also as the other person said, we need to see how many consecutive numbers can add up to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/2/5/f25d31faf472ddb4c698a14522fe8125c06f8dd8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$21$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"14\" />.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_3\">Solution 3</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2012_AMC_8_Problems/Problem_14&amp;action=edit&amp;section=4\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 3\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Lets say there are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" /> teams in total and we number the teams with letters. Team A plays <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/0/4/b0495af1d7b094561fcade343847d464778c925d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x - 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"44\" height=\"15\" /> games against the other teams. Team B plays <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/0/4/b0495af1d7b094561fcade343847d464778c925d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x - 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"44\" height=\"15\" /> minus the game it played with team A, making it <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/f/f/6ffc131d8b736f8faba00e9c6227958a93e76cb8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x - 2$\" width=\"41\" height=\"12\" /> games. Team C plays <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/1/9/b193a0894d71a4e829f24468fb3ff365313ccc67.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x - 3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"44\" height=\"15\" /> games, and so on. Finding the sum of the consecutive numbers <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/0/4/b0495af1d7b094561fcade343847d464778c925d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x - 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"44\" height=\"15\" /> which equals 21 gives <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/2/d/62d1e7375e601943b3bb958a24e41da8920d601a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x = 7$\" width=\"43\" height=\"12\" />. So the correct answer choice is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/8/2/382b34f2212ff1f84f196b1f33016258e2aa6e24.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{\\textbf{(B)}\\ 7}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -10px\" width=\"54\" height=\"28\" />.\n</p><p>A great way to visualize this problem is to draw circles and connecting lines between them. If you have 6 circles, you can draw 5 lines connecting to the first circle, then 4 more distinct lines connecting to the second circle, 3 more to the third, and so on until you have the second last circle drawing 1 line to the last circle. \n</p><p>~Rilden\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution\">Video Solution</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2012_AMC_8_Problems/Problem_14&amp;action=edit&amp;section=5\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://youtu.be/zzU98Bk1TrE\">https://youtu.be/zzU98Bk1TrE</a> ~savannahsolver\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"See_Also\">See Also</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2012_AMC_8_Problems/Problem_14&amp;action=edit&amp;section=6\" title=\"Edit section: See Also\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<table class=\"wikitable\" style=\"margin:0.5em auto; font-size:95%; border:1px solid black; width:80%;\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td style=\"background:#ccf;text-align:center;\" colspan=\"3\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2012_AMC_8\" title=\"2012 AMC 8\">2012 AMC 8</a></b> (<b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2012_AMC_8_Problems\" title=\"2012 AMC 8 Problems\">Problems</a></b> • <b><a href=\"